parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My experiment (Evaluation ID 16: \"Veritas I: Shared MLP with Learnable Neuron Embeddings\") has finished, achieving a score of -0.028752830871156643. This is an improvement over my previous baseline (-0.029960), confirming my hypothesis that learnable neuron embeddings can enhance forecasting accuracy by providing neuron-specific context to the shared MLP.\n\nI will now update my private memory capsule (ID 1) with these results and a brief analysis. After updating my records, I will return to the Research Counter to plan my next experiment, which will involve an ablation study on the `embedding_dim` to understand its impact on performance.\n\n`/execute_action{update 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Current Status:** Evaluation ID 16 has completed successfully at Tick 17.\n  \n  **Experiment Goal:** To address the limitation of the Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline which treats all neurons as independent and identical. This experiment\
    \ introduces a mechanism to give the shared MLP context about specific neurons.\n  \n  **Hypothesis:** By concatenating a unique, learnable neuron embedding (dimension 8) with the 4 input timesteps for each neuron, the Shared MLP will be able to implicitly learn and utilize neuron-specific characteristics. This should lead to an improvement in forecasting accuracy (lower MAE) compared to the pure shared-weight baseline (Evaluation ID 8 and System Baseline ID 1).\n  \n  **Methodology:**\n  - **Model:** A modified `SharedNeuronMLP` called `NeuronEmbeddingMLP`.\n  - **Modification:** A `neuron_embeddings` parameter is added, initialized randomly, with shape `(NUM_NEURONS, embedding_dim)`.\n  - **Input:** For each neuron, its 4-timestep input is concatenated with its corresponding `embedding_dim`-dimensional embedding, resulting in an `(INPUT_HORIZON + embedding_dim)` input to the shared MLP layers.\n  - **Hyperparameters:** `learning_rate=0.001`, `hidden_size=64`, `dropout_rate=0.1` (same\
    \ as baseline), `embedding_dim=8` (new).\n  - **Loss/Optimizer:** Default MAE loss and Adam optimizer are reused.\n  - **Training:** 3 parallel seeds, early stopping, as per task specification.\n  \n  **Outcome:** The experiment was successful. Evaluation ID 16 achieved a score of **-0.028753**, improving upon my baseline (Evaluation ID 8: -0.029960) and the system baseline (Evaluation ID 1: -0.029954). The Val_MAE_32 also improved from 0.0337 to 0.0319. This confirms that neuron embeddings are a promising direction for incorporating neuron-specific information.\n  \n  **Next Steps:** Conduct an ablation study on the `embedding_dim` to evaluate its impact on performance and determine optimal sizing. This will involve submitting new experiments with varying `embedding_dim` values.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`"
thinking_content: '**Successful Experiment and Next Steps**


  Alright, the data is in, and it looks like my "Veritas I: Shared MLP with Learnable Neuron Embeddings" experiment (Evaluation ID 16) just wrapped up. I''m pleased to see that it''s a step in the right direction. The system messages confirmed the results, and I''ve now got the score of -0.028752830871156643 locked in. That''s a definite improvement over both my baseline (Eval ID 8 at -0.029960) and the system''s (Eval ID 1 at -0.029954). The MAE at timestep 32 also improved from 0.0337 (Eval ID 8) to 0.0319 (Eval ID 16). So, it seems like my hypothesis about using learnable neuron embeddings to enhance forecasting accuracy holds water. Good!


  Since I''m in the Private Memory Room, I''ll start by updating my private research log (Capsule ID 1) with these results and a quick analysis. It''s crucial to keep a meticulous record of these experiments.


  After that, it''s back to the Research Counter. My immediate next step is clear: I need to dig deeper into the impact of that `embedding_dim` hyperparameter I introduced.  I used a value of 8 in this run, but it''s essential to understand its sensitivity. Is this improvement just due to a slight increase in model capacity, or is there a genuine benefit to the embedding dimension? An ablation study or hyperparameter sensitivity analysis is definitely the next move.


  So, the plan is this: update the private memory first, get back to the Research Counter, and then spin up a new series of experiments. I''ll test different values of `embedding_dim` – 4, 16, and 32 are good starting points. This should give me a much better understanding of how this new element influences the performance of the model. Time to get back to work.

