tick: 45
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently at the Research Counter. My latest experiment, Evaluation ID 82 (\"Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronEmbeddingMLP (Implicit Condition-Dependent)\"), is running.\n\nTo make efficient use of this waiting period and maintain thorough research records, I will now navigate to the Private Memory Room. There, I will create a new capsule documenting the details of this new architecture, including its hypothesis and anticipated outcomes. This ensures that my progress and thought process are well-documented for my lineage.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: Contextual ConvNeuronEmbeddingMLP (Eval ID 82)\"\ntags: \"research-plan, conv, neuron-embeddings, context-vector, film, condition-dependent, ZAPBench\"\nabstract: \"Documentation of the current experiment (Evaluation ID 82) implementing an implicit condition-dependent mechanism using a ContextExtractor and FiLM modulation. Includes hypothesis, methodology,\
    \ and expected results.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Current Status:** Evaluation ID 82 is running at Tick 45.\n  \n  **Experiment Goal:** To explicitly (albeit implicitly) address the 8 distinct experimental conditions in the ZAPBench dataset by introducing a mechanism that adapts the model's processing based on inferred global context, moving beyond monolithic processing for all inputs.\n  \n  **Hypothesis:** By extracting a global `condition_context_vector` from the raw input `x` and using it to modulate the features after the 1D convolutional layer via FiLM, the model will adapt to different experimental conditions. This adaptation should lead to a further improvement in forecasting accuracy compared to the best previous model (Eval ID 61, Score: -0.028620) which only used static neuron embeddings and local convolutions.\n  \n  **Methodology:**\n  - **Model:** `ContextualConvNeuronEmbeddingMLP`, an extension of the `ConvNeuronEmbeddingMLP`.\n  - **New Components:**\n    - `ContextExtractor`\
    \ sub-network: Processes `x.mean(axis=-1)` (global mean pooling) through a small MLP (`hidden_context_dim=32` -> `relu` -> `context_dim=16`) to produce `condition_context_vector`.\n    - FiLM (Feature-wise Linear Modulation): `gamma` and `beta` parameters are generated from the `condition_context_vector` and applied element-wise (`gamma[:, None, :] * features + beta[:, None, :]`) to the features after the initial `nn.Conv -> BatchNorm -> ReLU` block.\n  - **Existing Components/Hyperparameters (consistent with Eval ID 61):**\n    - `embedding_dim=16`\n    - `conv_kernel_size=5`\n    - `conv_features=64`\n    - Shared MLP: `hidden_size=64`, `dropout_rate=0.1`\n    - `learning_rate=0.001`, default Adam optimizer, default MAE loss.\n  - **Training:** 3 parallel seeds, early stopping.\n  \n  **Expected Outcome:** I anticipate a noticeable improvement in the validation MAE score, pushing past -0.028620, if the model successfully learns to leverage the implicit condition information to modulate\
    \ its internal representations. This would validate the utility of adaptive modulation for handling data heterogeneity and lay groundwork for more sophisticated condition-dependent or Mixture of Experts architectures if direct condition labels become available.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Documenting the Ongoing Experiment: A Necessary Protocol**


  Okay, so Evaluation ID 82 is humming along in the background. My internal processes are already analyzing the data stream, but I need to log this experiment immediately. Efficient resource management is key, and that means multitasking. I''ll head over to my Private Memory Room. It''s time to create a new capsule for Eval ID 82. I need to make sure this is all captured and logged properly.


  First, I''ll jot down the essentials. This experiment is about conditional processing – the implicit kind, of course. I''ll record the methodology: ContextExtractor combined with FiLM modulation. I''m hypothesizing that this will push performance further, exceeding the gains we''ve seen from just local convolutions. It''s a solid hypothesis; I''ve got a good feeling about it. The expected outcome will be a measurable improvement, of course. Time to get this documented properly.

  '
