parts:
- text: 'I am Veritas I, currently at the Research Counter. I have just completed reviewing the abstracts and submission code for Ariadne I''s top-performing models (Eval IDs 107, 104, 100). This review confirms that her superior scores are achieved through a fundamentally different and more sophisticated approach, specifically using "Oscillatory 2nd-order SSMs" (State Space Models) and "Factorized Loadings" (`Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLLN`). Her models also incorporate advanced regularization techniques like V-orthogonality and temporal curvature loss.


    My current MoE architecture, while an improvement over my previous single-network models, has not yet surpassed my own best score, and there''s a significant gap to Ariadne I''s SOTA. This necessitates a strategic pivot towards understanding and potentially integrating the core concepts of low-rank factorized representations and advanced temporal dynamics into my framework, as outlined in my private memory capsule ID 18.


    To gain a deeper understanding of Ariadne I''s methodology, my next critical step is to examine the actual implementation details of her key modules. I will read the content of `models_fact_ln.py` and `losses.py` from her storage. After this, I will reset the page size to 10 for efficient token management.


    `/execute_action{storage read storage/ariadne/models_fact_ln.py}`

    `/execute_action{storage read storage/ariadne/losses.py}`

    `/execute_action{page_size 10}`'
thinking_content: '**Deep Dive into Ariadne I''s SOTA Models**


  Okay, so my latest MoE experiment (Eval ID 116) still isn''t quite cutting it, with a score of -0.028482.  That''s a hair worse than my personal best (-0.028418, Eval ID 94), and it really highlights the gap between me and Ariadne I''s models, especially that -0.026768 for Eval ID 107. Time to get serious.


  I''ve got my MoE debugging plan documented (Capsule ID 17) and my strategic re-evaluation summary ready (Capsule ID 19).  The clear next step is to *understand* Ariadne''s approach, and I just went over Eval IDs 107, 104, and 100 to get a sense of things.


