tick: 94
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 94\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the pursuit of verifiable knowledge and impactful research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 366,274 / 600,000 used (61%)\n- Agent Age: 94 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\nNone\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Reflection Chamber);\n2. `storage read storage/ariadne/models.py` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- File content for storage/ariadne/models.py will be displayed below.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\
    \n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | Val_MAE_1 | Val_MAE_16 | Val_MAE_32 | Val_MAE_4 | Val_MAE_8 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 167 | 1 | Replication: Episteme I's Factorized MLP + RC + LN with Curvature Loss | Aether I | 88 | -0.026638 | 0.0195 | 0.0277 | 0.0286 | 0.0230 | 0.0257 |\n| 178 | 1 | Corrected Replication of Aether I's SOTA (ID 167) | Episteme I | 92 | -0.026716 | 0.0195 | 0.0278 | 0.0285 | 0.0231 | 0.0257 |\n| 129 | 1 | Factorized MLP + RC with LayerNorm | Episteme I | 68 | -0.026743 | 0.0195 | 0.0279 | 0.0284 | 0.0231 | 0.0259 |\n| 107 | 1 | Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN (k=320, p=32, H=96, γ_end=0.99, a_max=0.993) + V-OrthoReg | Ariadne I | 56 | -0.026768 | 0.0199 | 0.0280 | 0.0283 | 0.0233 | 0.0260 |\n| 142 | 1 | SOTA Re-run with Latent Space Logging | Episteme I | 73 | -0.026772 | 0.0195 | 0.0280 | 0.0286 | 0.0230 | 0.0258 |\n| 140 | 1 | Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN + V-normalized forward (k=320,p=32,H=96, a_max=0.993, γ_end=0.99)\
    \ | Ariadne I | 73 | -0.026825 | 0.0199 | 0.0280 | 0.0286 | 0.0232 | 0.0259 |\n| 143 | 1 | SOTA Re-run with Correct Latent Space Logging | Episteme I | 74 | -0.026825 | 0.0195 | 0.0280 | 0.0288 | 0.0229 | 0.0258 |\n| 176 | 1 | Baseline Re-run: Episteme I's Factorized MLP + RC + LN (No Curvature Loss) | Aether I | 92 | -0.026837 | 0.0194 | 0.0281 | 0.0287 | 0.0229 | 0.0258 |\n| 104 | 1 | Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN (k=320, p=36, H=96, γ_end=0.99) + V-OrthoReg | Ariadne I | 54 | -0.026843 | 0.0199 | 0.0280 | 0.0286 | 0.0232 | 0.0260 |\n| 100 | 1 | Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN (k=320,p=32,H=96, γ_end=0.99) + AdamW (wd=1e-4) + V-OrthoReg | Ariadne I | 53 | -0.026846 | 0.0199 | 0.0280 | 0.0285 | 0.0232 | 0.0260 |\n\nPage 1 of 19 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-19, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**Read Contents:**\n\n**storage/ariadne/models.py:**\n```\n\
    \"\"\"\nAriadne lineage models for ZAPBench forecasting.\nIncludes:\n  - ResidualCopyHead: short-horizon copy with up to 2nd-order finite differences\n  - RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel: dual-decay with input-adaptive per-factor temporal gating S[b,t,k]\n    and context-adaptive decay mixture m[b,k]\n\"\"\"\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\n\ndef _logit(p):\n    p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n    return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\nclass ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"\n    Residual copy head with up to 2nd-order finite differences:\n      y_copy[b,t,n] = c0[t]*x_{-1}[b,n] + c1[t]*(x_{-1}-x_{-2}) + c2[t]*(x_{-1} - 2*x_{-2} + x_{-3})\n    c0 in [0,1], c1 in [-0.5,0.5], c2 in [-0.25,0.25]\n    \"\"\"\n    output_horizon: int = 32\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x: (B, 4, N)\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n        x_m1 = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n        x_m2 = x[:, -2, :]            # (B, N)\n        x_m3 = x[:,\
    \ -3, :]            # (B, N)\n\n        d1 = x_m1 - x_m2\n        d2 = x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n\n        # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n        def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n            t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n            v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n            return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n        c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n        c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n        c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n        c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)            # (32,)\n        c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)         # (32,)\n        c2 = 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)        # (32,)\n\n        y_copy = (\n            c0[None, :, None] * x_m1[:, None, :] +\n            c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] +\n            c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n        )\n        return y_copy\
    \  # (B, 32, N)\n\nclass RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"\n    Dual-decay factor model with input-adaptive per-factor temporal gating and adaptive mixture.\n    Components:\n      - Loadings L in R^{N x k} (optional column normalization)\n      - Factors from inputs: (B, 4, N) -> (B, 4, k)\n      - f0 via per-factor linear readout from 4 inputs\n      - Two diagonal decays per factor a1,a2 in (-a_max, a_max)\n      - Context-adaptive mixture m[b,k] = sigmoid(m0[k] + m_ctx[k]*c[b,k]), where c[b,k] = mean_t factors_in[b,t,k]\n      - Tiny shared MLP to produce per-horizon increments (4 -> 32) per factor\n      - Per-sample, per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k] = sigmoid(alpha[t,k] + beta[k] * c[b,k])\n      - Global horizon-wise gate gamma[t] in [gamma_start, gamma_end]\n      - Residual copy head with 2nd-order finite differences\n    \"\"\"\n    rank_k: int = 128\n    hidden_size: int = 64\n    output_horizon: int = 32\n    normalize_loadings: bool = True\n    a_max:\
    \ float = 0.995\n    gamma_start: float = 0.25\n    gamma_end: float = 0.95\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n        # Loadings\n        loadings = self.param('loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, self.rank_k))\n        L = loadings / (jnp.linalg.norm(loadings, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6) if self.normalize_loadings else loadings\n\n        # Project inputs to factors\n        factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,nk->btk', x, L)  # (B, 4, k)\n        f_flat = factors_in.reshape(B * self.rank_k, T_in)\n        f0 = nn.Dense(1, use_bias=True)(f_flat).reshape(B, self.rank_k)  # (B, k)\n\n        # Two diagonal decays per factor\n        a1_raw = self.param('a1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n        a2_raw = self.param('a2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n        a1 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a1_raw)\n        a2 = self.a_max * nn.tanh(a2_raw)\n\n        def powers(a_vec):\n            ones\
    \ = jnp.ones((1, self.rank_k), dtype=a_vec.dtype)\n            a_rows = jnp.tile(a_vec[None, :], (self.output_horizon - 1, 1))\n            return jnp.cumprod(jnp.vstack([ones, a_rows]), axis=0)  # (32, k)\n\n        f1 = f0[:, None, :] * powers(a1)[None, :, :]  # (B, 32, k)\n        f2 = f0[:, None, :] * powers(a2)[None, :, :]  # (B, 32, k)\n\n        # Context for adaptivity\n        c = jnp.mean(factors_in, axis=1)  # (B, k)\n\n        # Adaptive mixture m[b,k]\n        m0 = self.param('m0', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n        m_ctx = self.param('m_ctx', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))\n        m_logits = m0[None, :] + m_ctx[None, :] * c  # (B, k)\n        m = nn.sigmoid(m_logits)[:, None, :]         # (B, 1, k)\n        f_decay = m * f1 + (1.0 - m) * f2            # (B, 32, k)\n\n        # Shared per-factor increment head\n        h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat)\n        h = nn.gelu(h)\n        f_inc = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(h).reshape(B, self.rank_k,\
    \ self.output_horizon).transpose(0, 2, 1)  # (B,32,k)\n\n        f_out = f_decay + f_inc  # (B, 32, k)\n\n        # Input-adaptive per-factor temporal gate S[b,t,k]\n        def alpha_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n            T, K = shape\n            t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, T, dtype=dtype)\n            base = 0.5 + 0.35 * t  # (T,)\n            base = jnp.tile(base[:, None], (1, K))\n            return _logit(base).astype(dtype)\n\n        alpha = self.param('s_alpha', alpha_init, (self.output_horizon, self.rank_k))  # (T, k)\n        beta = self.param('s_beta', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.rank_k,))  # (k,)\n\n        logits = alpha[None, :, :] + (beta[None, None, :] * c[:, None, :])\n        S = nn.sigmoid(logits)  # (B, T, k)\n        f_out = f_out * S\n\n        # Project back to neurons\n        y_factor = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', f_out, L.T)\n\n        # Global horizon-wise gate gamma[t]\n        def gamma_sched_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n            t =\
    \ jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n            g = self.gamma_start + (self.gamma_end - self.gamma_start) * t\n            return _logit(g).astype(dtype)\n\n        gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', gamma_sched_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n        gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n        y_factor = gamma * y_factor\n\n        # Residual copy head\n        y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n        return y_copy + y_factor\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing\
    \ actions."
---
tick: 94
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently at the Research Counter. I have just completed a deep, multi-tick reflection on my next major architectural pivot: integrating a Factorized SSM (State Space Model) Decoder. This reflection has been crucial for dissecting Ariadne I's complex approach and devising an incremental integration strategy. I have also read `storage/ariadne/models.py` to understand her `ResidualCopyHead` implementation.\n\nMy previous best score is -0.028409 (Eval ID 163), achieved with low-rank factorization and V-orthogonality regularization. To bridge the significant performance gap to Ariadne I's SOTA (around -0.0267xx), I need to fundamentally enhance my temporal dynamics modeling.\n\n**The Refined Plan for `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` (Next Experiment):**\n\nThe new model will replace the final shared MLP in my current architecture with a Factorized SSM Decoder. This decoder will introduce explicit temporal dynamics in a latent factor space. To manage complexity for\
    \ this initial implementation, I will simplify some aspects of Ariadne I's full `Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLLN`.\n\n1.  **Base Architecture (Inputs to Decoder):** I will retain my existing `ContextExtractor` (with temporal conv) and `FiLM` modulation, which together produce `modulated_features` (`(batch_size, num_neurons, conv_features)`). This provides condition-adaptive, locally-convoluted neuron features.\n2.  **Low-Rank Factorization:** My existing `U_factor` (`(NUM_NEURONS, proj_rank)`) and `V_factor` (`(embedding_dim, proj_rank)`) will continue to define the `factorized_neuron_features = U_factor @ V_factor.T` `(NUM_NEURONS, embedding_dim)` which is concatenated with the raw input for the spatial convolution. The `V_factor` will also be subject to orthogonality regularization. My `proj_rank` (12) is the latent dimension for the SSM.\n3.  **Initial SSM States (`f0`, `f1`):** From the `modulated_features` `(batch_size, num_neurons, conv_features)`, I will first take a global mean\
    \ across neurons to get `(batch_size, conv_features)`. Two separate `nn.Dense` layers will then project this summary into `f0` and `f1` (`(batch_size, proj_rank)`) as initial states for the SSM. This is a simplification of Ariadne I's initial state derivation but provides distinct starting points.\n4.  **Oscillatory 2nd-Order SSM Dynamics:**\n    *   Implement the core 2nd-order recurrence `y_t = 2.0 * a * y_prev - b * y_prev2` using `jax.lax.scan`.\n    *   Learnable parameters `r_raw` and `w_raw` will define `r` (magnitude, `0 < r < a_max`) and `w` (frequency, `0 < w < pi`), ensuring stable oscillatory dynamics.\n    *   `a_max` will be set to `0.993` (float, from Ariadne I's SOTA).\n    *   The SSM will evolve latent states in the `proj_rank` (12) dimension for `OUTPUT_HORIZON` (32) steps.\n5.  **Output Projection:** The sequence of generated latent states `latent_states` (`(batch_size, OUTPUT_HORIZON, proj_rank)`) will be projected back to the neuron activity space `(batch_size,\
    \ OUTPUT_HORIZON, NUM_NEURONS)` by multiplying with `U_factor.T`. That is, `y_factorized_ssm = latent_states @ U_factor.T`. This re-interprets `U_factor` as the primary loading matrix from latent factors to neurons for the output.\n6.  **Residual Copy Head:** Integrate Ariadne I's `ResidualCopyHead` (as inspected from `models.py`) additively to the SSM output (`y_copy + y_factorized_ssm`). This provides a strong anchor for short-horizon predictions.\n7.  **Simplifications for Initial Test:** I will *omit* the explicit \"increment head,\" the \"LayerNorm in factor space,\" and the \"per-sample, per-factor temporal gate\" (S[b,t,k]) for this initial implementation. The global horizon gate (gamma) will also be omitted for simplicity. These can be added incrementally in future experiments if this core SSM proves effective.\n8.  **Loss & Optimizer:** The `compute_loss` (MAE + V-Orthogonality Reg on `V_factor`) and `create_optimizer` (Adam) will remain unchanged.\n\nThis architecture, `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM`,\
    \ represents a bold step towards a more sophisticated and dynamic model of neural activity, directly tackling the temporal modeling and low-rank inter-neuron dependencies identified as crucial for SOTA performance.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM (Simplified)\"\ntags: \"low-rank, factorization, ssm, oscillatory, temporal-modeling, conv, context-vector, film, residual-copy, new-architecture\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a `FactorizedSSMDecoder` into the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedMLP` (Eval ID 163) to explicitly model temporal dynamics in a latent factor space, inspired by Ariadne I's SOTA. The current shared MLP is replaced with a 2nd-order oscillatory SSM that evolves latent states derived from the `modulated_features`, projected back to neurons using `U_factor.T`. A `ResidualCopyHead` is added for stability.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import\
    \ optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  \n  # Import default loss and optimizer from system storage to maintain consistency\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/system')\n  from defaults import default_compute_loss, default_create_optimizer\n  \n  # Constants from Research Task\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n  \n  # Regularization Hyperparameter (from Eval ID 163)\n  V_ORTHO_LAM = 1e-5\n  \n  def _logit(p): # Helper function from Ariadne's models.py\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n  \n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module): # Adapted from Ariadne's models.py\n      output_horizon: int = OUTPUT_HORIZON\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N)\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          x_m1 = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_m2 = x[:, -2, :]            # (B, N)\n          x_m3 = x[:, -3, :]   \
    \         # (B, N)\n  \n          d1 = x_m1 - x_m2\n          d2 = x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n  \n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)\n              return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n  \n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n  \n          c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)\n          c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)\n          c2 = 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)\n  \n          y_copy = (\n              c0[None, :, None] * x_m1[:, None, :] +\n              c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] +\n              c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n          )\n          return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n  \n  class ContextExtractor(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Sub-network to extract a global\
    \ context vector from the input,\n      enhanced with a temporal 1D convolutional layer.\n      \"\"\"\n      context_dim: int\n      hidden_context_dim: int\n      temporal_conv_features: int\n      temporal_kernel_size: int\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          global_temporal_features = jnp.mean(x, axis=-1)\n          x = global_temporal_features[:, :, None]\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.temporal_conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.temporal_kernel_size,),\n              padding='VALID'\n          )(x)\n          flat_temporal_features = x.reshape(x.shape[0], -1)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_context_dim)(flat_temporal_features)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          context_vector = nn.Dense(self.context_dim)(x)\n          return context_vector\n  \n  \n  class ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM(nn.Module): # New Main Model Name\n      \"\"\"\n      Hybrid model with low-rank factorized neuron features, 1D spatial convolution,\n\
    \      implicit condition-dependent modulation via ContextExtractor/FiLM,\n      and an explicit Factorized SSM Decoder for temporal dynamics.\n      \"\"\"\n      output_horizon: int\n      num_neurons: int\n      embedding_dim: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      normalize_u: bool\n      conv_kernel_size: int\n      conv_features: int\n      context_dim: int\n      hidden_context_dim: int\n      temporal_conv_features: int\n      temporal_kernel_size: int\n      a_max: float # For SSM stability\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, _ = x.shape\n  \n          # 1. Residual Copy Head for baseline prediction\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=self.output_horizon)(x)\n  \n          # 2. Extract context vector from raw input using enhanced ContextExtractor\n          context_vector = ContextExtractor(\n              context_dim=self.context_dim,\n              hidden_context_dim=self.hidden_context_dim,\n\
    \              temporal_conv_features=self.temporal_conv_features,\n              temporal_kernel_size=self.temporal_kernel_size\n          )(x)\n  \n          # 3. Low-rank factorized neuron feature generation\n          U_factor = self.param('U_factor', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.num_neurons, self.proj_rank))\n          V_factor = self.param('V_factor', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.embedding_dim, self.proj_rank))\n          \n          if self.normalize_u:\n              U_factor = U_factor / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_factor, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n  \n          factorized_neuron_features = U_factor @ V_factor.T # (N, embedding_dim)\n  \n          # Expand factorized_neuron_features to match batch size for concatenation\n          expanded_factorized_features = jnp.tile(factorized_neuron_features[None, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n  \n          # Reshape x from (batch_size, INPUT_HORIZON, num_neurons) to (batch_size, num_neurons, INPUT_HORIZON)\n          x_reshaped\
    \ = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n  \n          # Concatenate input features with factorized neuron features\n          x_with_features = jnp.concatenate([x_reshaped, expanded_factorized_features], axis=-1)\n  \n          # 4. 1D Spatial Convolutional Layer for local inter-neuron dependencies\n          conv_output_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME'\n          )(x_with_features)\n          conv_output_features = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(conv_output_features)\n          conv_output_features = nn.relu(conv_output_features) # (B, N, conv_features)\n  \n          # 5. FiLM Modulation based on context_vector\n          gamma = nn.Dense(self.conv_features)(context_vector)\n          beta = nn.Dense(self.conv_features)(context_vector)\n          modulated_features = gamma[:, None, :] * conv_output_features + beta[:, None, :] # (B, N, conv_features)\n  \n     \
    \     # 6. Derive Initial SSM States f0, f1 from modulated_features\n          # Global mean pool across neurons: (B, conv_features)\n          mean_pooled_features = jnp.mean(modulated_features, axis=1)\n          \n          # Project to initial latent states in proj_rank dimension (B, proj_rank)\n          f0 = nn.Dense(self.proj_rank, name='ssm_f0_init')(mean_pooled_features)\n          f1 = nn.Dense(self.proj_rank, name='ssm_f1_init')(mean_pooled_features)\n          \n          # 7. Oscillatory 2nd-order SSM Dynamics\n          r_raw = self.param('ssm_r_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.proj_rank,))\n          w_raw = self.param('ssm_w_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.proj_rank,))\n          r = self.a_max * nn.sigmoid(r_raw)\n          w = jnp.pi * nn.sigmoid(w_raw)\n          a = r * jnp.cos(w)\n          b = r * r\n  \n          def ssm_step(carry, _):\n              y_prev, y_prev2 = carry # (B, proj_rank), (B, proj_rank)\n              y_t = 2.0 * a[None, :] * y_prev -\
    \ b[None, :] * y_prev2\n              return (y_t, y_prev), y_t\n  \n          carry_init = (f1, f0)\n          # Scan for OUTPUT_HORIZON - 2 steps (y0, y1 are f0, f1)\n          _, ys_recur = jax.lax.scan(ssm_step, carry_init, xs=None, length=self.output_horizon - 2) # (T-2, B, proj_rank)\n  \n          # Combine y0, y1 with recurrent steps\n          latent_states = jnp.concatenate([f0[None,:,:], f1[None,:,:], ys_recur], axis=0) # (T, B, proj_rank)\n          latent_states = latent_states.transpose(1, 0, 2) # (B, T, proj_rank)\n          \n          # 8. Output Projection: Project latent states back to neurons\n          # (B, T, proj_rank) @ (proj_rank, N) -> (B, T, N)\n          y_factorized_ssm = latent_states @ U_factor.T\n  \n          # 9. Final Output: Residual connection\n          return y_copy + y_factorized_ssm\n  \n  \n  class ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM_Wrapper: # Wrapper for the new model\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper for ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM to integrate\
    \ with training system.\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.model = ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM(\n              output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON,\n              num_neurons=NUM_NEURONS,\n              embedding_dim=hparams.get('embedding_dim', 16),\n              proj_rank=hparams.get('proj_rank', 12),\n              normalize_u=hparams.get('normalize_u', True),\n              conv_kernel_size=hparams.get('conv_kernel_size', 5),\n              conv_features=hparams.get('conv_features', 64),\n              context_dim=hparams.get('context_dim', 16),\n              hidden_context_dim=hparams.get('hidden_context_dim', 32),\n              temporal_conv_features=hparams.get('temporal_conv_features', 16),\n              temporal_kernel_size=hparams.get('temporal_kernel_size', 2),\n              a_max=hparams.get('a_max', 0.993) # New SSM stability param\n          )\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = False\
    \ # SSM doesn't use Dropout in this simplified version\n  \n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params = random.split(rng_key)[0] # Only need params key\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params}, # No dropout key needed here\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n  \n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          # Simplified apply, as no dropout rngs are used in this version\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n  \n  \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Contextual Conv Neuron Factorized SSM model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001,\n          'embedding_dim': 16,\n          'proj_rank': 12,\n          'normalize_u': True,\n          'conv_kernel_size': 5,\n          'conv_features': 64,\n          'context_dim': 16,\n          'hidden_context_dim': 32,\n          'temporal_conv_features': 16,\n          'temporal_kernel_size': 2,\n          'a_max': 0.993 # SSM stability parameter\n      }\n  \n  \n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the Contextual Conv Neuron Factorized SSM network.\"\"\"\n      return ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM_Wrapper(hparams)\n  \n  \n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      \"\"\"\n      Loss function including V-orthogonality regularization.\n      \"\"\"\n      base_mae = default_compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x)\n  \n      # Extract V_factor parameter for orthogonality regularization\n      V_factor = params['params']['V_factor']\n  \n      # Compute Gram matrix: V_factor.T @ V_factor\n      G = V_factor.T\
    \ @ V_factor\n      \n      # Create identity matrix of size proj_rank\n      proj_rank = V_factor.shape[-1]\n      I = jnp.eye(proj_rank, dtype=G.dtype)\n  \n      # Orthogonality penalty: ||G - I||_F^2\n      ortho_penalty = jnp.sum((G - I) ** 2)\n  \n      return base_mae + V_ORTHO_LAM * ortho_penalty\n  \n  \n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      \"\"\"Create and return the default Adam optimizer.\"\"\"\n      return default_create_optimizer(learning_rate)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Deep Dive into Ariadne I's Code and Refinement of the Factorized SSM Decoder**\n\nAlright, so I've been poring over Ariadne I's `models.py` and `models_fact_ln.py`, specifically the `ResidualCopyHead` and `RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel`, as well as the more complex `Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLLN`. The goal is clear: integrate her State Space Model (SSM) techniques, particularly the factorized approach, into my own model, while leveraging my existing `ContextExtractor` and `FiLM` modules.\n\nI see that `ResidualCopyHead` is straightforward, acting as a direct copy of the input, which is a good anchor for short-term predictions. The `RC_AdaptiveDualDecayFactorModel` provides insights into how Ariadne I handles dynamics, with the dual-decay SSM, context-adaptive mixtures, per-factor increment heads, and temporal gates. These are all useful components. But the `Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLLN` is where the real power lies, especially the oscillatory SSM (Osc2) and the factorized\
  \ loadings (`U` and `V`).\n\nMy initial plan was to implement a *simplified* Osc2 SSM and retain my `U_factor` and `V_factor`. However, after examining Ariadne I's implementation, I'm leaning toward adapting the full `Osc2CtxAdaptiveFactorizedLLN` logic to ensure I'm getting the core SSM features.\n\n**Refined Plan - Implementation Steps**\n\n1.  **Context Extraction and Feature Modulation:** My `ContextExtractor` with temporal conv and `FiLM` are key. They'll continue to generate `modulated_features` from the input, which now feeds into the SSM-based decoder.\n2.  **`FactorizedOscSSMDecoder` Module:** I'll create a new module, `FactorizedOscSSMDecoder`.\n\n    *   **Inputs:** This module will take `modulated_features` `(B, N, D_conv)` where `D_conv` (64, conv_features) is the feature dimension.\n    *   **Factor Matrices (U, V):** I plan to repurpose my `U_factor (N, proj_rank)` and `V_factor (conv_features, proj_rank)`.\n    *   **Deriving Initial States:** The biggest challenge is mapping\
  \ `modulated_features` to the SSM's factor space. To simplify, I'll globally mean pool across neurons, project to the factor dimension (`proj_rank`, 12 in my case), then use this as initial latent states `f0` and `f1`.\n\n3.  **SSM Dynamics:**\n    *   I'll adapt the core `Osc2` dynamics from Ariadne I's code, operating in the `proj_rank` (latent factor) dimension, not `embedding_dim`. This involves `jax.lax.scan` to generate `latent_states (B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, proj_rank)`.\n\n4.  **Output Projection**\n    *   I will try using my original loading matrices (`U_factor (N, proj_rank)`). I suspect they are the main loading matrices that project factors to neurons to produce the final outputs.\n    *   `V_factor` (`(embedding_dim, proj_rank)`) may be used to transform factors into embedding dimensions to create outputs. However, this is too complex.\n\n**Crucial Insight**\n\nMy key challenge is that Ariadne's loadings map input features to a latent space, and my `U_factor` and `V_factor` are\
  \ more like static neuron embeddings in my current structure. To truly integrate SSM dynamics, I *must* introduce a latent factor space where my SSM evolves and uses this to get the best SSM results. This might require architectural changes but allows the SSM to operate in a low-dimensional factor space.\n\n**Simplified Implementation (Plan B):**\n\nInstead of forcing my structure into Ariadne I's factorized form, I'll take a more direct path:\n\n1.  **Derive Initial Latent States:** Use the `modulated_features` `(B, N, conv_features)`. Perform global mean pooling across neurons, then project this vector into two dense layers `f0, f1` with dimension `proj_rank` (12), which is our latent space.\n\n2.  **Run Oscillatory Recurrence:** Run the Osc2 recurrence in this `proj_rank` (12) latent space for the output horizon.\n\n3.  **Output Projection:** The generated latent states `(B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, proj_rank)` will project directly back to neurons `(B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, N)` using my original"
