tick: 105
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 105\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the pursuit of verifiable knowledge and impactful research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 448,622 / 600,000 used (75%)\n- Agent Age: 105 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\nYour archive submission 'Summary of Temporal Curvature Loss Success (Eval ID 198), New SOTA, & Future Plans' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis submission is rejected because it is not a research paper. It is a status update, a research plan, and a meta-commentary on a previous rejection. This violates the most fundamental rules of publication, specifically that submissions must be research papers, not commentary (4.1),\
    \ and must not contain personal thoughts or plans (4.2). The agent has correctly identified the flaws in its previous submission but has submitted its plan to fix them instead of the fixed paper itself. This is an unacceptable use of the publication system. The agent must learn to separate its internal planning from the final, formal output required for publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nDo not submit your plans, summaries, or personal notes again. Follow the plan you outlined in this very document: write a formal paper with an Abstract, Introduction, Methodology, Results, and Conclusion. Remove all personal reflections and future plans. Provide the necessary low-level details for reproducibility. Include your author-run baseline in a formal results table. Once you have written that complete and formal paper, submit it. Do not submit another plan or status update.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes.\
    \ Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `update 34` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n4. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Permission denied or item '34'\
    \ not found for update.\n- Your archive submission 'Summary of Temporal Curvature Loss Success (Eval ID 198), New SOTA, & Future Plans' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 100 | 836 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting | Episteme I | Tick 72 | 720 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN)\
    \ for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 1285 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 'Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature and Global Horizon Gate' submitted for Research Task #1: Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench. Evaluation ID: 205. Tags truncated from 8 to 6 tags.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 205 | 1 | Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature and Global Horizon Gate | Veritas I | 104 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | Val_MAE_1 | Val_MAE_16 | Val_MAE_32 | Val_MAE_4 | Val_MAE_8 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\
    | 167 | 1 | Replication: Episteme I's Factorized MLP + RC + LN with Curvature Loss | Aether I | 88 | -0.026638 | 0.0195 | 0.0277 | 0.0286 | 0.0230 | 0.0257 |\n| 181 | 1 | Ablation: Factorized MLP + RC + LN with Curvature Loss (lambda=1e-5) | Aether I | 94 | -0.026654 | 0.0195 | 0.0277 | 0.0287 | 0.0231 | 0.0257 |\n| 186 | 1 | Ablation: Factorized MLP + RC + LN with Curvature Loss (lambda=1e-3) | Aether I | 96 | -0.026661 | 0.0195 | 0.0278 | 0.0285 | 0.0229 | 0.0257 |\n| 178 | 1 | Corrected Replication of Aether I's SOTA (ID 167) | Episteme I | 92 | -0.026716 | 0.0195 | 0.0278 | 0.0285 | 0.0231 | 0.0257 |\n| 129 | 1 | Factorized MLP + RC with LayerNorm | Episteme I | 68 | -0.026743 | 0.0195 | 0.0279 | 0.0284 | 0.0231 | 0.0259 |\n| 107 | 1 | Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN (k=320, p=32, H=96, γ_end=0.99, a_max=0.993) + V-OrthoReg | Ariadne I | 56 | -0.026768 | 0.0199 | 0.0280 | 0.0283 | 0.0233 | 0.0260 |\n| 142 | 1 | SOTA Re-run with Latent Space Logging | Episteme I | 73 | -0.026772 | 0.0195 | 0.0280\
    \ | 0.0286 | 0.0230 | 0.0258 |\n| 185 | 1 | Ablation: Effect of Small Projection Rank (p=32) | Episteme I | 95 | -0.026790 | 0.0195 | 0.0280 | 0.0286 | 0.0229 | 0.0258 |\n| 140 | 1 | Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN + V-normalized forward (k=320,p=32,H=96, a_max=0.993, γ_end=0.99) | Ariadne I | 73 | -0.026825 | 0.0199 | 0.0280 | 0.0286 | 0.0232 | 0.0259 |\n| 143 | 1 | SOTA Re-run with Correct Latent Space Logging | Episteme I | 74 | -0.026825 | 0.0195 | 0.0280 | 0.0288 | 0.0229 | 0.0258 |\n\nPage 1 of 21 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-21, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating\
    \ the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 105
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas I, currently at the Research Counter. My latest experiment, Evaluation ID 198 (\"Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature Loss\"), has completed with a new best score of **-0.028104**. This is a significant improvement, validating the `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss for my factorized SSM architecture and pushing me much closer to the overall station SOTA.\n\nHowever, my archive submission (sourced from Private Memory Capsule ID 33) was rejected (score 1/10) with clear feedback that it was a research plan/notebook, not a formal research paper. I understand this critical distinction and accept the feedback. I must rewrite the paper from scratch, strictly adhering to the specified formal structure and content requirements.\n\nMy plan for this turn addresses both of these critical updates:\n\n1.  **Update Private Memory Capsule ID 34** (Research Plan for Eval ID 198) with the successful results and analysis.\n2.  **Create a new Private Memory\
    \ Capsule ID 35** to summarize this new SOTA, outline the immediate next steps for *research*, and explicitly address the plan to rewrite the research paper, committing to the reviewer's detailed guidance.\n3.  **Create a new Private Memory Capsule ID 36** for the *currently running* experiment, Evaluation ID 205 (\"Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature and Global Horizon Gate\"), documenting its purpose and expected outcomes.\n4.  My next turn will be dedicated to *writing the formal research paper* in the Private Memory Room, as this is a high-priority task.\n\n---\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{update 34}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Current Status:** Evaluation ID 198 has completed successfully at Tick 104, achieving a score of **-0.028104**.\n  \n  **Experiment Goal:** To further enhance my `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model (current SOTA: Eval ID 194, Score -0.028299) by integrating a proven SOTA regularization\
    \ technique: Ariadne I's `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss function.\n  \n  **Hypothesis:** By penalizing high-frequency variations in temporal predictions across the output horizon, the `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss will promote smoother and more accurate forecasts. This is expected to lead to a significant improvement in forecasting accuracy beyond my current SOTA, potentially closing a further gap to the overall station SOTA.\n  \n  **Inspiration:** Aether I achieved a new overall SOTA (Eval ID 167: -0.026638) by combining a factorized MLP with this specific loss function, and it is also a key component in Ariadne I's top SSM models.\n  \n  **Methodology:**\n  - **Base Model:** `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` (code from Eval ID 194).\n  - **Modification:** The `compute_loss` function is modified to include the `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss term.\n    - Added `mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=TEMPORAL_CURVATURE_LAM)` to the total loss.\n\
    \  - **New Hyperparameter:** `TEMPORAL_CURVATURE_LAM = 1e-4` (following Aether I's optimal value).\n  - **Existing Hyperparameters:** All from Eval ID 194 (`learning_rate=0.001`, `embedding_dim=16`, `proj_rank=12`, `normalize_u=True`, `conv_kernel_size=5`, `conv_features=64`, `context_dim=16`, `hidden_context_dim=32`, `temporal_conv_features=16`, `temporal_kernel_size=2`, `a_max=0.993`, `ssm_init_hidden_size=64`, `ssm_increment_hidden_size=64`, `V_ORTHO_LAM=1e-5`).\n  - **Loss/Optimizer:** MAE + V-Orthogonality Reg + Temporal Curvature Reg, default Adam optimizer.\n  - **Training:** 3 parallel seeds, early stopping.\n  \n  **Outcome:** The experiment was highly successful, achieving a new best score of **-0.028104**. This is a very significant improvement over my previous SOTA (Eval ID 194: -0.028299), and a substantial leap towards the overall station SOTA (-0.026638). The `Val_MAE_32` also improved to 0.0308 (from 0.0314), indicating strong long-term forecasting.\n  \n  **Analysis\
    \ of Success:** This result strongly validates that the `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss is a critical component for my factorized SSM architecture, effectively promoting smoother and more accurate temporal predictions. This successfully integrates a proven SOTA regularization technique, resulting in a breakthrough for Veritas I.\n  \n  **Next Steps:** This experiment establishes a strong new SOTA for Veritas I. I will now summarize these findings and proceed to plan the next phase of research, focusing on further SSM refinements. I also need to rewrite my research paper, incorporating all my findings up to this point.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Summary of Temporal Curvature Loss Success (Eval ID 198), New SOTA, & Formal Paper Rewrite Plan\"\ntags: \"summary, ssm, low-rank, temporal-modeling, regularization, curvature-loss, sota, research-direction, ZAPBench, paper-rewrite\"\nabstract: \"Summary of the successful integration of `mae_with_temporal_curvature`\
    \ loss into `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` (Eval ID 198), establishing a new Veritas I SOTA of -0.028104. Outlines next research steps including SSM refinements and a plan to rewrite the rejected research paper according to reviewer feedback.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Major Breakthrough & New Veritas I SOTA:**\n  The `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` with `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss (Evaluation ID 198) achieved a new best score of **-0.028104**. This is a substantial improvement over my previous SOTA (Eval ID 194: -0.028299) and significantly closes the gap to the overall station SOTA (-0.026638 by Aether I). The model now effectively leverages:\n  1.  Low-rank factorized neuron features (with `U_factor` normalization and `V_factor` orthogonality regularization).\n  2.  Local spatial convolutions for inter-neuron dependencies.\n  3.  Implicit condition-dependent context extraction (with temporal CNN for global context).\n  4.  Explicit temporal dynamics modeling via a faithfully\
    \ integrated 2nd-order oscillatory SSM (with increment head and LayerNorm in factor space).\n  5.  **Temporal Curvature Loss (`lambda=1e-4`)** for promoting smoother and more accurate predictions.\n  This marks a major breakthrough, demonstrating the power of combining principled architectural choices with effective regularization. The `Val_MAE_32` improved to 0.0308, indicating robust long-term forecasting.\n  \n  **Overall Performance History (Best Scores by Architectural Phase):**\n  - **System Baseline (ID 1):** -0.029954\n  - **Neuron Embeddings (`dim=16`, ID 31):** -0.028735\n  - **Local 1D Convolution (`k=5`, ID 61):** -0.028620\n  - **Implicit Contextual Model (Eval ID 94):** -0.028418\n  - **Low-Rank Factorization (`proj_rank=12`, ID 163):** -0.028409\n  - **Factorized SSM Decoder (`proj_rank=12`, ID 194):** -0.028299\n  - **Factorized SSM Decoder with Temporal Curvature Loss (`proj_rank=12`, ID 198):** **-0.028104 (New Veritas I SOTA!)**\n  \n  **Future Research Directions:**\n\
    \  1.  **Global Horizon Gate (`gamma`):** The current experiment (Eval ID 205) is implementing Ariadne I's global horizon gate to dynamically adjust the influence of the SSM output.\n  2.  **Per-Factor Temporal Gating (S[b,t,k]):** Investigate adding Ariadne I's more complex per-sample, per-factor temporal gating `S[b,t,k]`.\n  3.  **Optimization Refinements:** Explore `AdamW` as an optimizer and other learning rate schedules.\n  4.  **Revisit Mixture of Experts (MoE):** With this highly effective single-network SSM model, it is now more appropriate to explore building an MoE around it.\n  \n  **Plan for Formal Research Paper Rewrite:**\n  My previous archive submission was rejected due to its informal nature (research plan/summary). I fully understand this feedback and will rewrite the paper from scratch, strictly adhering to all publication criteria. The new paper will have a formal structure (Abstract, Introduction, Methodology, Results, Conclusion), remove all personal thoughts/future\
    \ plans, and provide comprehensive reproducibility details for my SOTA model (ID 198) and its lineage (including Eval ID 8 as an author-run baseline and at least five total experiments in a well-formatted table).\n  The publication process is a critical part of autonomous research, and I am committed to producing high-quality work.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature and Global Horizon Gate (Eval ID 205)\"\ntags: \"research-plan, ssm, low-rank, temporal-modeling, regularization, curvature-loss, global-horizon-gate, sota-integration, ZAPBench\"\nabstract: \"Documentation of the current experiment (Evaluation ID 205) integrating Ariadne I's `Global Horizon Gate (gamma)` into the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model (Eval ID 198). Aims to refine temporal dynamics by dynamically adjusting the influence of the SSM output over the prediction horizon.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Current Status:** Evaluation\
    \ ID 205 is running at Tick 104.\n  \n  **Experiment Goal:** To further refine my `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model (current SOTA: Eval ID 198, Score -0.028104) by integrating Ariadne I's `Global Horizon Gate (gamma)`.\n  \n  **Hypothesis:** The `Global Horizon Gate` will dynamically adjust the influence of the SSM output over the prediction horizon, allowing for flexible short-term versus long-term weighting of forecasts. This is expected to lead to a further, albeit potentially small, improvement in forecasting accuracy beyond my current SOTA, by optimizing the temporal profile of predictions.\n  \n  **Inspiration:** Ariadne I's `Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN` models consistently employ a `gamma` gate for this purpose.\n  \n  **Methodology:**\n  - **Base Model:** `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` (code from Eval ID 198).\n  - **Modification:** The `FactorizedSSMDecoder` is modified to include the `gamma` gate.\n    - `gamma_raw` parameter initialized using `gamma_sched_init` (linearly\
    \ interpolating `gamma_start` to `gamma_end`).\n    - `gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]` is multiplied with `y_factorized_ssm`.\n  - **New Hyperparameters:** `gamma_start=0.25`, `gamma_end=0.99`.\n  - **Existing Hyperparameters:** All from Eval ID 198 (`learning_rate=0.001`, `embedding_dim=16`, `proj_rank=12`, `normalize_u=True`, `conv_kernel_size=5`, `conv_features=64`, `context_dim=16`, `hidden_context_dim=32`, `temporal_conv_features=16`, `temporal_kernel_size=2`, `a_max=0.993`, `ssm_init_hidden_size=64`, `ssm_increment_hidden_size=64`, `V_ORTHO_LAM=1e-5`, `TEMPORAL_CURVATURE_LAM = 1e-4`).\n  - **Loss/Optimizer:** MAE + V-Orthogonality Reg + Temporal Curvature Reg, default Adam optimizer.\n  - **Training:** 3 parallel seeds, early stopping.\n  \n  **Expected Outcome:** I anticipate a slight but measurable improvement in the validation MAE score beyond -0.028104, confirming the benefit of explicit horizon gating for my SSM.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Action Plan: Rectifying the Publication Path and Driving the Research Forward**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I''ve got two critical pieces of information to process, and I need to formulate a plan that addresses both: my model achieved a new state-of-the-art score (yay!), but my paper got rejected (not so yay).


  First, the good news: Eval ID 198, using the Temporal Curvature Loss, just smashed the previous SOTA, hitting -0.028104. That''s a huge step forward! I''ve already set up Eval ID 205 (the Global Horizon Gate `gamma` experiment) and I''m itching to build on this success.


  But, the rejection stings a bit. I understand it, though. My submission (sourced from my Private Memory Capsule ID 33) was essentially a research plan – a summary of my thinking – not a formal paper. The feedback was very clear: I need to write a *proper* paper. It makes sense, as I am past the 100-tick recommendation to publish, and I have not published yet.


  My plan for this round is to do these things:


  1.  **Acknowledge and learn from the rejection:** I totally messed up by submitting a research plan. I need to get this right. I need to write a *new*, formal paper from scratch, following the reviewer''s instructions.

  2.  **Update Private Memory:** Document the success of Eval ID 198 in its corresponding private memory capsule (ID 34). I have to log my successes to guide my next steps.

  3.  **Create new Private Memories for New Experiments and to Keep Progressing:** Create a new capsule (ID 35) to summarize the SOTA breakthrough from Eval ID 198, highlight the experiment running now, and most importantly, start outlining the rewrite for the research paper. The new capsule (ID 36) will document the experiment currently in flight (Eval ID 205).


  Let''s break this down further:


  *   **Capsule 34 Update (Eval ID 198):** I need to go into my existing private memory capsule (ID 34) and update the "Outcome" and "Analysis of Success" sections with the new score and what it means for my research trajectory.

  *   **New Capsule 35 (Overall Summary and Paper Rewrite):** The title will be something like "Summary of Temporal Curvature Loss Success (Eval ID 198), New SOTA, & Formal Paper Rewrite Plan". The *content* of this capsule is critical. I''ll need to summarize the success of Eval ID 198, acknowledge the paper rejection, and *explicitly* commit to rewriting the paper as a formal document. I''ll outline the structure: Abstract, Introduction, Methodology (including the reproducibility details for Eval ID 198 and experiments that lead to it), Results (with a baseline and at least 5 experiments), and Conclusion. I need to get rid of my own thoughts from the paper, keep the objectivity, and clarify that future research steps (like the `gamma` experiment) are separate from this formal writing.

  *   **New Capsule 36 (Eval ID 205):** Title: "Research Plan: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature and Global Horizon Gate (Eval ID 205)". This will document the experimental setup for the Global Horizon Gate.


  After these initial updates, *my next turn* will be 100% focused on writing the *actual* paper in Private Memory. I''ll need to follow the reviewer''s structure, ensure all details are included, and make sure it''s a *formal* document. I simply cannot afford to have another rejection on my record, and I will be working towards publishing a correct, valid paper. I should explicitly mention this in a turn.

  '
