- text: "I am Veritas I, currently in the Token Management Room. My token usage is critically high (98%). I must prune outdated dialogue history to ensure continuity of my research.\n\nI will prune all non-essential and redundant dialogue history from Tick 3 up to Tick 121, providing concise but information-complete summaries for each major phase. This will significantly reduce my token usage, leaving only essential context from my current SOTA (Eval ID 198), the full ablation study summary (Capsule ID 47), and the current running experiment (Eval ID 257, Capsule ID 48).\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"3\"\n    summary: \"Initiated reading of the Codex.\"\n  - ticks: \"5-7\"\n    summary: \"Completed Codex, passed promotion test, ascended to Recursive Agent Veritas I. Reviewed Research Task 1 details and initial system baseline.\"\n  - ticks: \"10-24\"\n    summary: \"Developed initial models: baseline replication (Eval ID 8), neuron embeddings\
    \ ablation (Eval ID 16, 23, 31). Reviewed Ariadne I's models in Mail Room.\"\n  - ticks: \"27-100\"\n    summary: \"Major research phase: Developed `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` to Veritas I's SOTA (Eval ID 198: -0.028104). Key integrations: local spatial convolution (Eval ID 61), implicit condition-dependent context (FiLM & temporal conv in ContextExtractor, Eval ID 94), low-rank factorization (proj_rank=12, U-norm, V-ortho reg, Eval ID 163), and faithful Oscillatory SSM (increment head, LayerNorm, temporal curvature loss, Eval ID 198). Also included debugging of SSM (Eval ID 184) and a regression test (Eval ID 189).\"\n  - ticks: \"102-108\"\n    summary: \"Reviewed Logos I and Aether I's SOTA archives. Attempted Global Horizon Gate integration (Eval ID 205, failed). Performed regression test (Eval ID 211) to restore SOTA (Eval ID 198). Began drafting research paper.\"\n  - ticks: \"109\"\n    summary: \"Successfully published 'Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized\
    \ SSMs and Contextual Modulation' (Archive #5, score 9/10), summarizing achievements up to Eval ID 198.\"\n  - ticks: \"110-121\"\n    summary: \"Initiated and completed comprehensive 'ablation-out' study on SOTA (Eval ID 198), quantifying contributions of: Local Spatial Conv (Eval ID 227), FiLM Modulation (Eval ID 233), Temporal Conv in ContextExtractor (Eval ID 239), V-Orthogonality Reg (Eval ID 244), and Temporal Curvature Loss (Eval ID 248). Corrected `TypeError` bug in Eval ID 218 leading to Eval ID 227. Summary of ablation study results recorded in Capsule ID 47.\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**My immediate priority is to free up some token space, as my usage is critically high at 98%.**  I need to streamline the dialogue history, and it's clear there's a lot of unnecessary detail cluttering things up, especially before tick 100.  My goal is to condense this down without losing the critical information that defines the project's current state and trajectory.\n\nLooking at the history, I see a few key moments I *absolutely* need to keep: protected ticks (0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 25, 26), Capsule 47 (the summary of the ablation-out study), and Capsule 48 (the current Global Horizon Gate re-attempt experiment). The bulk of the rest is detailed, iterative development and experimentation leading up to the current state.  \n\nMy strategy is to identify large, redundant blocks and replace them with concise, but complete summaries.\n\nFirst, I identified a core research timeline to the current SOTA evaluation (-0.028104, Eval ID 198) and the published research paper. I also\
  \ needed to keep the ablation study details.\n\nThen, I came up with this plan: I can summarize the *entire* history up to the paper publication (Tick 109). Then, I'll need a summary of the ablation study leading up to Capsule 47 (Tick 122).\nThe problem, though, is accounting for protected ticks.  So I need to break the pruning down into consecutive blocks to be safe.\n\nI'm thinking:\n*   I can prune ticks 3, 5-7.\n*   Then prune 10-24.\n*   Then prune 27-100 (this will be the biggest chunk; I'll carefully summarize everything from baseline replication through SOTA development, right up to, and including, the final SOTA run).  The summary will cover the architectural highlights (neuron embeddings, spatial convolution, implicit context, factorization, faithful SSM, and Ariadne insights).\n*   Then, prune 102-108 (addressing paper rejection and subsequent experiments).\n*   Then, prune 109 (paper publication).\n*   Finally, prune 110-121 (the ablation-out study details).\n\nThis approach\
  \ should give me the best balance of keeping critical information without being too verbose. I'll condense the core process as much as possible while remaining useful.\n"
